## Issues

- [x] `src/tools/init/scaffoldCommands/index.ts` lines 28–43: `COMMANDS` registry has no entry for `update-playbook`. The spec requires the fixed list to include the maintenance command alongside the seven pipeline phases. No `{ canonical: "commands/aide/update-playbook", hostPath: "aide/update-playbook.md", displayName: "aide:update-playbook" }` entry exists.
  Traces to: `outcomes.desired[0]` — "plus the update-playbook maintenance command"
  Misalignment: implementation-drift

- [x] `src/tools/init/initContent/index.ts` lines 34–60: `DOC_PATHS` registry has no entry for `"commands/aide/update-playbook"`. `readCanonicalDoc` would throw (or fail to compile) on that key. The path registration must exist before the `COMMANDS` entry can resolve.
  Traces to: `outcomes.desired[0]` — update-playbook must be installable through initContent
  Misalignment: implementation-drift

- [x] `.claude/commands/aide/`: no `update-playbook.md` file exists. This is the canonical template that `readCanonicalDoc` reads at runtime and that gets byte-faithfully installed into host projects. Without the source file, the entire update-playbook scaffold chain is broken from template through initContent through scaffoldCommands. The template must also contain the drift-detection housekeeping step (desired outcome [5]) — its absence means that requirement cannot currently be satisfied even if the other two registration gaps were closed.
  Traces to: `outcomes.desired[1]` — byte-faithful render of canonical template; `outcomes.desired[5]` — update-playbook includes drift-detection housekeeping step; `outcomes.undesired[7]` — update-playbook missing drift detection
  Misalignment: plan-gap

- [x] `.claude/skills/study-playbook/SKILL.md` lines 28–29: Step 1 reads "The hub starts with a **Task Routing** table that maps task domains to sections. Match your current task against the table to identify which 1–3 sections apply." This names a specific artifact ("Task Routing" table), asserts where it appears (top of hub), and constrains the result to a count ("1–3 sections"). All three are environment-specific assumptions. The spec requires zero such references — crawling mechanics only.
  Traces to: `outcomes.desired[4]` — "zero environment-specific content: no intent mapping tables, no task-to-section routing, no references to specific playbook section names"; `outcomes.undesired[6]` — skill must not contain task-to-section routing
  Misalignment: implementation-drift

- [x] `.claude/skills/study-playbook/SKILL.md` line 30: "Do NOT read all sections — only the ones the routing table points you to." The phrase "the routing table" presupposes that a named routing table is the operative navigation artifact. This is an implicit structural assumption. Generic crawling protocol should describe how to traverse hub links, not name a specific artifact the user's hub may not have.
  Traces to: `outcomes.desired[4]`; `outcomes.undesired[6]`
  Misalignment: implementation-drift

- [x] `.claude/skills/study-playbook/SKILL.md` lines 29, 33–35: Step 2 uses path examples `coding-playbook/services/services.md` and `coding-playbook/services/error-handling.md`. These are specific to the author's playbook layout. A user with a different vault structure sees path examples that do not match their actual organization and may misapply the instructions. All concrete path examples must be removed or replaced with structural placeholders.
  Traces to: `outcomes.desired[4]` — zero environment-specific content; `outcomes.undesired[6]`
  Misalignment: implementation-drift

## Retro

**What would have caught these earlier?**

Issues 1–3 (update-playbook not wired) would have been caught at the **plan stage** if the plan included an explicit three-part checklist matching the spec's three-way lock: (1) canonical template file exists, (2) `DOC_PATHS` entry registered, (3) `COMMANDS` entry added. The spec names this invariant explicitly in the strategy section — "all three changes land in the same PR or none of them do" — but no plan item enforced it. A plan that maps directly to the three-way lock surface would have surfaced the gap before any implementation started.

Issues 4–6 (environment-coupled skill) would have been caught at the **spec stage** if the "zero environment-specific content" outcome had existed before the skill was last edited. The skill predates this spec revision. Future revisions to desired outcomes that constrain existing canonical files should trigger a QA pass on those files immediately — the spec change and the file change should be a single unit of work, not a deferred follow-up.

A lightweight QA heuristic for skill templates: grep for hardcoded path fragments (containing `/`), named artifacts in quotes, and numeric constraints on section counts. Any of those patterns in a skill that claims to be generic is a candidate finding.
